**Reused evidence**
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0009"],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

**New evidence**
- [NATO press conference transcript](https://www.nato.int/en/news-and-events/events/transcripts/2022/06/28/press-conference) is the cleanest 2022 record of Erdoğan choosing compromise in a major security dispute, with the Finland/Sweden memorandum addressing Türkiye’s concerns and opening NATO accession.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press conference | NATO Transcript","publisher":"NATO","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.nato.int/en/news-and-events/events/transcripts/2022/06/28/press-conference","claim":"The trilateral memorandum addressed Türkiye's concerns, including arms exports and counterterrorism, and Türkiye agreed to support Finland and Sweden joining NATO.","locator":"L816-L830","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary NATO transcript with direct, dated statements from the summit press conference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Erdoğan because he participated in the trilateral meeting and the memo was concluded with his assent.","contrary_evidence":["This was a multilateral bargain, so it shows compromise rather than unilateral peacemaking."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- [Sweden government readout on the Permanent Joint Mechanism](https://www.government.se/articles/2022/11/meeting-of-the-permanent-joint-mechanism--turkiye-finland-and-sweden/) shows the June memorandum was still being implemented in November, which matters for whether the compromise had durable follow-through.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Meeting of the Permanent Joint Mechanism – Türkiye, Finland and Sweden","publisher":"Government.se","publication_date":"2022-11-25","url":"https://www.government.se/articles/2022/11/meeting-of-the-permanent-joint-mechanism--turkiye-finland-and-sweden/","claim":"The Permanent Joint Mechanism was intended to ensure implementation of the Trilateral Memorandum, and the parties reported progress on technical cooperation and compliance.","locator":"L399-L400","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government readout of the trilateral mechanism with a clear implementation statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant to Erdoğan because Türkiye was the constraining party in the memorandum and the mechanism tracked delivery.","contrary_evidence":["A mechanism can exist without proving that the underlying security bargain was fully implemented in practice."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

- [UN Commission of Inquiry report on Syria](https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/2078691/G2246309.pdf) captures the key 2022 escalation trigger from Erdoğan himself: the planned-incursion announcement was followed by military escalation and mutual bombardment.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Report of the Independent International Commission of Inquiry on the Syrian Arab Republic","publisher":"United Nations Human Rights Council","publication_date":"2022-08-17","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/2078691/G2246309.pdf","claim":"The Commission said that President Erdogan's announcement of another planned incursion was followed by episodes of military escalation, mutual bombardment, and mobilization by all parties.","locator":"L59-L61","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN commission report based on first-hand interviews, documents, photos, videos, and satellite imagery.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Erdoğan's public announcement and the ensuing Turkish military posture.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes a genuine Turkish threat environment from Da'esh and other armed actors."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.10"]}

- [HRW question-and-answer on the threatened incursion](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/08/17/turkey/syria-civilians-risk-northern-syria) is the strongest warning that Erdoğan’s 2022 Syria plan risked repeating earlier abuses and civilian displacement.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey/Syria: Civilians at Risk in Northern Syria","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-08-17","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/08/17/turkey/syria-civilians-risk-northern-syria","claim":"HRW said Turkey's planned incursion would be its fourth since 2016 and that previous incursions had been fraught with human rights abuses, including killings, arbitrary arrests, and massive displacement.","locator":"L608-L613","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed rights analysis published on the day of the warning, with explicit laws-of-war framing and concrete prior-incursion examples.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Erdoğan because the threatened incursion was his stated policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["HRW is advocacy-oriented and assesses a threatened operation, not a fully executed 2022 ground invasion."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.5","2B.7"]}

- [HRW on 2022 Turkish strikes](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/12/07/northeast-syria-turkish-strikes-exacerbate-humanitarian-crisis) supplies the broader 2022 pattern: repeated shelling, artillery, and drone attacks that worsened the humanitarian and displacement picture.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Northeast Syria: Turkish Strikes Exacerbate Humanitarian Crisis","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/12/07/northeast-syria-turkish-strikes-exacerbate-humanitarian-crisis","claim":"HRW said Turkey repeatedly subjected northeast Syrian cities and towns to shelling, artillery, and drone attacks in 2022, and warned that the campaign aggravated humanitarian and displacement crises.","locator":"L634-L636","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights reporting on the 2022 strike pattern with explicit humanitarian consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Erdoğan's government and military command structure.","contrary_evidence":["The article is not a casualty audit and does not by itself establish a complete incident-by-incident legal finding."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.10"]}

- [Reuters on the November 2022 air campaign](https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/21/syria-security-turkey) is the core escalation source for Operation Claw-Sword, including the retaliation frame and competing civilian-harm claims.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkish air strikes target Kurdish militants in Syria, Iraq after bomb attack","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-11-21","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/21/syria-security-turkey","claim":"Turkey said its warplanes destroyed 89 targets in Syria and Iraq in retaliation for the Istanbul bomb attack, while the SDF alleged civilian deaths and damage to civilian infrastructure.","locator":"L659-L668","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting with direct contemporaneous government and opposing-side claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdoğan because the operation was a Turkish national-security decision announced by his government.","contrary_evidence":["Turkey said only terrorists were targeted; the SDF alleged civilian deaths and the destruction of grain silos, a power station, and a hospital."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.6","2B.9"]}

- [Reuters on Erdoğan’s land-operation threat](https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/24/syria-security-turkey) shows that the 2022 campaign was not framed as a one-off defensive raid but as an expandable cross-border offensive.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan says Turkey will launch Syria land operation when convenient","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/24/syria-security-turkey","claim":"Erdoğan said Turkey's air operations were only the beginning and that a land operation would be launched when convenient, while creating a security corridor in Syria and Iraq.","locator":"L662-L666","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report directly attributes the statement to Erdoğan in a dated parliamentary speech context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Erdoğan.","contrary_evidence":["He also claimed to respect Syria and Iraq's territorial integrity, so the statement mixes coercion with legalistic framing."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.3","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- [Reuters on strikes hitting oil installations](https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/25/syria-security-turkey) adds the strongest late-2022 contrary evidence because it links Turkish action to deeper territorial reach and alleged civilian harm.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkish strikes hit oil installations in northern Syria, sources say","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-11-25","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/25/syria-security-turkey","claim":"Reuters reported that Turkish drones targeted oil installations in northeast Syria, marking the first systematic Turkish targeting of oil fields there, while the SDF alleged civilian deaths and the Pentagon said the strikes threatened U.S. personnel.","locator":"L663-L669 and L682-L685","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting combines local-source claims, Turkish rebuttal, and U.S. reaction in one contemporaneous article.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly relevant to Erdoğan because it describes a Turkish military campaign ordered by his government.","contrary_evidence":["Turkey said oil fields were not targets and that it selected targets sensitively."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- [Anadolu Agency’s operation readout](https://www.aa.com.tr/en/turkiye/turkish-army-shares-video-of-fresh-military-operation-against-terrorist-group-pkk/2743648) is the clearest official justification record: self-defense under Article 51 and a public insistence that the targets were military.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkish army shares video of fresh military operation against terrorist group PKK","publisher":"Anadolu Agency","publication_date":"2022-11-20","url":"https://www.aa.com.tr/en/turkiye/turkish-army-shares-video-of-fresh-military-operation-against-terrorist-group-pkk/2743648","claim":"The National Defense Ministry said Operation Claw-Sword was carried out in line with the right of self-defense under Article 51 of the UN Charter after the Istanbul attack and that the operation targeted PKK/YPG hideouts and military structures.","locator":"L121-L127","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Turkish news agency account of the defense ministry's contemporaneous justification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant because it reflects the government's stated legal basis and operational narrative under Erdoğan.","contrary_evidence":["Official framing alone does not resolve whether the response was proportionate or whether civilian harm was adequately avoided."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.6","2B.8"]}

- [US State Department annual report on Syria](https://www.ecoi.net/de/dokument/2089061.html) captures the proxy/occupation problem around Turkish-backed forces, including abuses and no visible prosecution path.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Syria","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/de/dokument/2089061.html","claim":"Armed Syrian opposition groups supported by Turkey committed abuses including extrajudicial killings, unlawful detention, forced evacuations, looting, child soldier recruitment, and other violations, and there was no information available on prosecution of individual personnel.","locator":"L50-L52","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual U.S. government human-rights reporting summarizing multiple NGO and COI findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 reporting year, published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but important: the report ties the conduct to groups supported by Türkiye and, in some cases, to Turkish officials and effective control.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that the SDF committed abuses, so responsibility is not exclusive to Türkiye's proxies."],"lenses":["2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across NATO, UN, HRW, Reuters/Euronews mirrors, Turkish official outlets, US State reporting, and a few secondary explainers.
- I rejected several Reuters mirrors because they repeated the same article without adding new facts.
- I rejected later 2023-2024 retrospective pieces when they did not improve the 2022 evidence on Erdoğan’s actual choices.
- I rejected several Turkish government items that only repeated the same Operation Claw-Sword justification, unless they added a materially distinct claim or locator.
- I rejected broad background pages on Syria, NATO, or the PKK when they were purely contextual and not ruler-attributable for 2022.
- I did not keep weaker or blocked items that could not be opened cleanly or only restated already-captured claims.

**Remaining gaps**
- The biggest gap is direct internal decision evidence: public records do not show Erdoğan’s private deliberations, legal review, or command-chain specifics for civilian-harm mitigation.
- Proxy-control evidence is still incomplete for the exact Turkish relationship to every SNA faction in 2022, even though the proxy-abuse pattern is well documented.
- The public record is thin on any Turkish compensation, investigation, or discipline after the 2022 Syria strikes.
- More searching could still help on parliamentary, court, or oversight records, but it is unlikely to overturn the basic 2022 picture: real diplomatic restraint in some venues, paired with a substantial and escalating cross-border Syria/Iraq military campaign.
